  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was mixed across both stores, driven by capacity constraints at Port Washington and a scheduling cliff at Hicksville that needs immediate attention.\n\nPort Washington is firing on retention and retail but losing grooming productivity. You had six groomers yesterday producing $408 per groomer against a trailing baseline of $512\u2014a 20% efficiency gap. The real story: core groom revenue fell 13% week-over-week while add-on attach jumped from 28% to 59% and retail exploded up 159%. This tells us your team is upselling hard, but you're not running enough capacity to cover base demand. Month-to-date you're down 9.8% against DOW-adjusted expectation, which is real. One bright spot: your 12+ week visit-gap bucket is shrinking (15.5% to 13.3%), a leading indicator that retention discipline is working. You have 496 customers in lapse pool worth $317k in annual opportunity.\n\nHicksville is a different problem\u2014and more urgent. You're up 20.7% month-to-date (beating DOW-adjusted expectation handily), but yesterday your groomer productivity was $387 against a $409 baseline, and today and tomorrow you have just one groomer scheduled. That's a coverage crisis. Your visit-cadence bucket is growing (10.2% to 12.6%), signaling stealth churn. Hicksville needs a staffing plan, not a performance plan.\n\nPort Washington today: 6 groomers, 7.0h bathers. Hicksville today: 1 groomer, 0.0h bathers. Next 7 days: Port Washington 34 groomer-days (healthy), Hicksville 9 groomer-days (understaffed); you need to address Hicksville's schedule immediately\u2014one groomer cannot sustain momentum."
